hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1642","\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb: My uncle narrated to me: Yunus narrated to me, from al-Zuhri, who said: ‘Abbad ibn Ziyad narrated to me that ‘Urwah ibn al-Mughirah ibn Shu‘bah informed him that he heard his father say:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and I were together during the expedition of Tabuk before dawn. I accompanied him, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) dismounted to relieve himself. I poured water from a water-skin over his hands, and he washed his palms, then his face, then he uncovered his forearms. The sleeves of his garment were tight, so he put his hand in and pulled them out from under his garment, then washed his arms up to the elbows. He then wiped his head, performed wudu’ over his two leather socks, and mounted. We continued until we found the people already in prayer, having appointed ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn ‘Awf to lead them. He led them in one rak‘ah of the Fajr prayer. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) then stood and lined up with the Muslims, praying the second rak‘ah behind ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn ‘Awf. When ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn ‘Awf gave the salam, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood to complete his prayer. The Muslims were alarmed and increased their glorification (saying Subhan Allah), because they had preceded the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the prayer. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave the salam, he said to them, “You have done well, or you have attained correctness.”\n\n"
